diagnosis_text:
The reported fault — host_1_1_1_1 unable to reach the 10.200.0.0/24 server farm — is not present in the live lab and leaves no diagnostic trace at any layer.

**Affected component:** None found. Every subsystem on the alleged fault path was checked: L2 identity (no duplicate MACs, all interfaces UP), host addressing (host_1_1_1_1 holds 10.1.1.2 with a correct default gateway and no ARP poisoning), OSPF/FRR control plane (all six routers fully adjacent, 10 OSPF routes each, FRR daemons active), traffic control (no qdisc shaping on any of 30 scanned interfaces), ACL/firewall (no nftables or iptables drop rules anywhere), DNS (10.200.0.2 correctly answers all four web hostnames), and HTTP service (all four web servers return 200 to both client hosts, average 4–5 ms).

**Mechanism:** No fault mechanism is present. Direct reachability probes from host_1_1_1_1 to 10.200.0.4 and 10.200.0.6 show 0 % loss. HTTP GETs from host_1_1_1_1 to web0.local–web3.local all succeed. There is no flap process, no carrier-change history, no routing-table gap, and no service-layer anomaly.

**Localization:** No device or interface is implicated.

**Root cause:** The helpdesk symptom is unconfirmed — the current lab state is fully healthy and the 10.200.0.0/24 server farm is reachable from host_1_1_1_1 at all layers tested.

**Fix:** No corrective action is warranted. If the issue was transient, the network has self-recovered; no device needs to be restarted. If the user continues to experience problems, re-open the ticket with packet captures or syslog evidence gathered at the time of the failure.
